---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const prestonCfg = {
  points: [
    {
      points: [[1.7, 62], [6, 53.5], [9, 68], [16.5, 73.5], [21.5, 78.5], [43, 84.5]],
      color: '#0f766e',
      label: 'countries (2022)',
      mode: 'dots',
    },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 50, label: 'GNI per person, PPP $1000s' },
  y: { min: 45, max: 90, label: 'life expectancy (years)' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 350,
};

const hdiCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'le', label: 'life expectancy', min: 20, max: 85, step: 0.5, value: 73, unit: 'yrs' },
    { name: 'mys', label: 'mean years of schooling', min: 0, max: 15, step: 0.1, value: 8.7 },
    { name: 'eys', label: 'expected years of schooling', min: 0, max: 20, step: 0.1, value: 13 },
    { name: 'gni', label: 'GNI per capita (PPP)', min: 200, max: 90000, step: 100, value: 21000, unit: 'US$' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'health index', expr: '(le-20)/65' },
    { label: 'education index', expr: '(mys/15 + eys/18)/2' },
    { label: 'income index', expr: 'min(1, (ln(gni)-ln(100))/(ln(75000)-ln(100)))' },
    { label: 'HDI', expr: '(((le-20)/65) * ((mys/15 + eys/18)/2) * (min(1, (ln(gni)-ln(100))/(ln(75000)-ln(100)))))^(1/3)' },
  ],
};

const tradeCfg = {
  type: 'vector',
  unit: 'bn US$',
  x: { min: 0, max: 20 },
  y: { min: 0, max: 20 },
  start: [6, 9],
  height: 320,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/geography/intermediate/development-and-inequality">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Development is not just growth. <strong>Standard of living</strong> is what money buys —
    income, goods, housing; <strong>quality of life</strong> (syllabus 8.1.3) is how good
    living actually is — health, education, security, a clean environment. The two usually
    travel together but not always. To compare countries we use indicators (8.1.1):
    <strong>GDP</strong> and <strong>GNI per capita</strong>, literacy rate, life expectancy,
    infant mortality, doctors per person, calorie intake, and the composite
    <strong>Human Development Index</strong>. The syllabus sorts countries into
    <strong>LICs, MICs and HICs</strong> — while admitting, as we must, that the sorting is
    crude: a country can be middle-income by money and high-income by health.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the Preston-curve shape: richer means longer-lived — but with diminishing returns (UNDP HDR 2023/24 data, 2022, rounded). Niger 1.7/62; Nigeria 6/53.5; India 9/68; Brazil 16.5/73.5; China 21.5/78.5; Japan 43/84.5" widget="plotter" config={prestonCfg} />

  <p>
    Fig. 1 is development geography's core chart. Note two things: the curve flattens (the
    first few thousand dollars of GNI buy enormous life-expectancy gains; the next fifty
    thousand buy very few), and the scatter matters — the USA famously lives shorter than
    Japan despite far higher income per person.
  </p>

  <h2>The HDI, by hand</h2>
  <p>
    The HDI (8.1.2) combines three pillars with a geometric mean: health
    (life expectancy, rescaled 20→85 years), education (mean and expected years of
    schooling), and income (GNI per capita on a log scale, capped at $75,000). The widget
    below runs the real UNDP formula. Defaults land near 0.75 — between the world average
    (0.739) and Brazil (0.760). Plug in Niger's actual figures — life expectancy ~62, about 2
    years of schooling, ~$1,700 GNI per capita — and you get ≈ 0.40 against the published
    0.394. Range check for 2022: Switzerland tops the table at 0.967; South Sudan sits at
    0.381.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="compute a real HDI: the UNDP's own formula — watch how a log-scaled income term stops rich countries maxing out the index" widget="sliderLab" config={hdiCfg} />

  <p>
    Every indicator has limits (8.1.2). GDP per capita is an average — it hides inequality,
    the informal economy, unpaid care work, and what the output actually is (weapons or
    vaccines). The HDI adds health and education but still says nothing about the
    environment, rights, or who within the country gets the life it scores.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the gap — and what shape it is</h2>
  <p>
    Reasons for uneven development (8.2.1) come in three flavours.
    <strong>Historical-economic:</strong> colonial extraction built ports for exports, not
    economies; many LICs still depend on commodities — UNCTAD counts more than 80 countries,
    mostly in Africa and South America, earning over 60% of export income from commodities
    whose prices swing wildly. <strong>Physical:</strong> being landlocked (much of the
    Sahel), malarial, or drought-prone raises the cost of every step.
    <strong>Social-political:</strong> conflict destroys decades of progress; education gaps
    — especially for girls — throttle everything downstream. The pattern (8.2.2) is no longer
    a neat north–south line: HICs cluster in North America, western Europe and East Asia
    (Japan, South Korea, Singapore, Australia south of the equator); most of humanity lives
    in MICs (China, India, Brazil); LICs concentrate in Sub-Saharan Africa. And the gap runs
    <em>within</em> countries: in India, Kerala's health outcomes rival far richer nations
    while the northern plains lag behind.
  </p>

  <h2>Trade, aid, and closing the gap sustainably</h2>
  <p>
    Sustainable development (8.3.1) is the Brundtland definition: meeting today's needs
    without wrecking future generations' ability to meet theirs. Strategies (8.3.2) are
    social (education — each extra school year lifts earnings ~10%; vaccination), economic
    (diversification, industrialisation), and environmental (leapfrogging straight to
    renewables: Kenya generates ~90% of its electricity from geothermal, hydro and wind).
    Evaluate the classic trio (8.3.3): <strong>aid</strong> can save lives but risks
    dependency; <strong>debt relief</strong> — the HIPC scheme has freed more than $100
    billion across 36 countries — works when savings reach schools and clinics;
    <strong>trade</strong> is the engine that worked for East Asia, but only when the terms
    let value flow in. Drag the arrow below: exports on x, imports on y.
  </p>

  <Widget no="3" caption="a year of trade for a developing country: a 45° arrow is balanced; steep means importing more than export earnings cover — the classic LIC squeeze" widget="diagramBuilder" config={tradeCfg} />

  <p>
    The detailed example (8.3.4) is <strong>Vietnam</strong>, an MIC that explains itself:
    poor in 1986 (the Doi Moi reforms year), it opened to markets, invested ferociously in
    schooling and ports, and grew ~6–7% a year for decades. World Bank figures show poverty
    falling from nearly 60% in the early 1990s to under 5% today, and its HDI has climbed to
    0.726 — development raised sustainably by trade plus education, not aid.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE Geography 0460 syllabus (2027–2029), topic 8; UNDP Human Development Report 2023/24; World Bank Open Data; UNCTAD State of Commodity Dependence; Brundtland Report, Our Common Future (1987).</p>
</TopicPage>
